'''Mundari''' (<small>[[Mundari Bani]]:</small> {{lang|unr-Nagm|𞓧𞓟𞓨𞓜𞓕𞓣𞓚}}, <small>romanised:</small> ''Muṇḍārī'', [[International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]]: {{IPA|unr|muɳɖaːriː}}) is a [[Munda languages|Munda language]] of the [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic]] language family spoken by the [[Munda people|Munda]] tribes native to the [[Chota Nagpur Plateau]] region in [[India]] with over 1.5 million native speakers.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.omniglot.com/writing/mundari.htm|title=Mundari alphabet, pronunciation and description}}</ref> It is closely related to [[Ho language|Ho]] and [[Santali language|Santali]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://omniglot.com/writing/mundaribani.htm|title=Mundari Bani}}</ref> and along with [[Bhumij language|Bhumij]], is one of the four major Munda languages. Mundari is an additional official language in the state of [[Jharkhand]],<ref name="JharkhandOfficialLang2011">{{cite news |last1=Pramanik |first1=S. |title=Jharkhand notifies 11 languages as second official languages |url=https://www.thehindu.com/sport/races/velocita-pleases/article2411802.ece|work=[[The Hindu]]|date=2011-08-31 |access-date=22 February 2026}}</ref> and has significant speakers in eastern Indian states of [[Odisha]] and [[West Bengal]] and northern [[Rangpur Division]] of Bangladesh. In India, Mundari is recognised as a significant minority language. However, its speakers are often bilingual in [[Hindi]] or the local state language. 

Mundari is an [[agglutinative language]] characterised by its complex [[Morphology (linguistics)|morphology]], where multiple affixes are added to roots to convey grammatical relationships.<ref name="Andersen2008">{{cite book|last=Andersen |first=Gregory D. S.|title=The Munda Languages |publisher=Routledge|year=2008|isbn=978-0415328906|pages=121–150|chapter=Mundari}}</ref> While historically transmitted through oral tradition, Mundari is now written using several scripts, most notably [[Mundari Bani]], invented by [[Rohidas Singh Nag]] specifically to write Mundari.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.oneindia.com/2006/05/15/bms-to-intensify-agitation-on-mundari-language-1147760918.html|title=BMS to intensify agitation on Mundari language|website=oneindia.com|access-date=4 April 2018}}</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20161108183956/http://www.stscodisha.gov.in/pdf/June-Dec-2012-Vol-1-2.pdf "Adivasi. Volume 52. Number 1&2. June&December 2012".] Page 22</ref> It has also been written in the [[Devanagari]], [[Oriya script|Odia]], [[Bengali alphabet|Bengali]], and [[Latin script|Latin]] writing systems.

Mundari has been classified as a [[vulnerable language]] according to the [[UNESCO]] ''[[Atlas of the World's Languages in Danger]]''.<ref>{{cite book |last=Moseley|first=Christopher|editor-last=Moseley|editor-first=Christopher |title=Atlas of the World's Languages in Danger|edition=3rd|date=2010|publisher=UNESCO Publishing |location=Paris|isbn=978-92-3-104096-2 |url=https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000187026/PDF/187026eng.pdf.multi|format=PDF|page=205|access-date=22 February 2025}}</ref> It remains a subject of extensive linguistic study due to its unique grammatical structure and its role in understanding the migration patterns of [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic speakers]] in [[South Asia]].

==History==
The term ''Muɳɖa'' means "village headman" in Mundari.{{sfn|Osada|2008|p=99}} Neighboring communities of the Mundas referred to their language as ''Muɳɖārī'', and the Mundas themselves call it ''hoɽo dʒagar'' ("human language"; ''hoɽo''–"man", ''dʒagar''–"to speak, speech") or ''muɳɖa dʒagar'' ("Munda language").{{sfn|Osada|2008|p=99}} Studies on Mundari started in the nineteenth century, pioneered by the works of Haldar (1871), Whitley (1873), and Nottrott (1882), though most of them were brief sketches and documentations. Then in 1903, German missionary/linguist [[John-Baptist Hoffmann|John Hoffmann]] initiated two massive and influential projects on Mundari: ''Mundari Grammar'' (1903–1905) and ''[[Encyclopaedia Mundarica]]'' (1903–1978), the latter was completed long after his death and was published posthumously.{{sfn|Osada|2008|p=100}}

